### 4.2 Requirement Traceability Matrix
Requirements and user stories will be mapped dynamically in Jira and linked to version-controlled test assets so that every acceptance criterion has a corresponding manual or automated validation record. For AD-488, the trace model will begin with the story description and explicit acceptance criteria, then expand into test conditions by session state, file type, file size boundary, malware outcome, storage result, CDN result, and UI refresh result. Each condition will be assigned a unique test identifier and linked to the originating acceptance objective, enabling transparent coverage reporting during change review, sprint review, and release-readiness assessment.

Operationally, traceability will be maintained through a combination of Jira issue links, test case management references, pull-request references, and CI execution outputs. Every automation script must reference the story key or mapped test identifier in its metadata or naming convention, and every manual execution artifact must be attached to the corresponding Jira test execution or linked evidence record. When requirements change, the QA owner will perform impact analysis by identifying all linked tests, reviewing affected automation, and reclassifying unaffected, modified, or newly required coverage. This practice prevents silent drift between the intended business rule set and the actual release evidence.

The traceability discipline is especially important for this feature because multiple controls enforce the same acceptance area. For example, authentication affects both UI visibility and gateway rejection; file validation affects both extension handling and header inspection; successful completion depends on storage, CDN purge, and UI synchronization. The trace matrix will therefore support one-to-many and many-to-one mappings rather than forcing a simplistic one-row-per-story view. This is essential for auditability, regression selection, and efficient failure triage.

### 4.3 Test Management
Test management for this initiative will be run through Jira for workflow governance, with linked test assets stored in the source repository and execution evidence captured in CI and test management records. The team will operate a disciplined lifecycle covering test design, review, approval, execution, defect logging, re-test, regression, and closure. Each planned scenario will be classified by risk level, automation candidacy, and execution phase so that the delivery team can distinguish between fast merge-blocking checks and deeper release-certification suites. The QA lead will maintain the execution dashboard daily during active validation, publishing coverage, pass/fail status, blocked items, defect severity distribution, and environment health.

From an operating model perspective, all new or modified test cases and automation scripts must undergo peer review before being marked ready. Pull requests impacting avatar upload behavior must include linked Jira references, test evidence for changed acceptance criteria, and reviewer confirmation that relevant negative paths were considered. The CI/CD pipeline will enforce minimum controls: unit/configuration checks at branch level, API and UI smoke validations on merge, nightly integrated regressions in QA, and pre-release end-to-end plus SLA validation in pre-production. Human-in-the-loop approval remains mandatory before promotion from validated build to release candidate, ensuring that execution data, residual defects, and operational risks are consciously reviewed rather than implicitly accepted by automation alone.

Defect triage will be integrated into the test management cycle. Daily triage sessions involving QA, engineering, and product will classify failures as blocker, critical, high, medium, or low, with explicit root-cause domain tagging such as UI, gateway, security validation, malware scan, storage, CDN, or environment. Reopened defects will be treated as trend indicators and reviewed for missing regression coverage. Exit from test execution will require completion of all planned critical-path scenarios, closure or accepted disposition of severe defects, and documented sign-off that observed residual risk aligns with release tolerance. This operational rigor is necessary because the feature crosses several services, and seemingly minor failures can have large user-visible or security consequences.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
All test materials will be stored in the enterprise GitHub repository structure, with functional and end-to-end automation maintained under version-controlled folders aligned to service boundaries and test layers. UI automation assets for browser behavior and dynamic DOM assertions will reside with the front-end quality suite, API and validation checks will reside within the service-quality or shared integration automation package, and performance assets will be versioned alongside release engineering scripts to preserve execution repeatability. Repository ownership will be shared between QA automation maintainers and designated engineering reviewers, with branch protections enforcing pull-request review, status checks, and auditability for all changes.

Tracking updates will be maintained by linking repository commits and pull requests back to Jira story AD-488 and any derivative defect or sub-task records. Test asset naming conventions will include functional scope, execution layer, and trace identifier so that reviewers can immediately understand coverage intent. Release branches will inherit only approved and reviewed test changes, and emergency updates to test assets will follow the same change-control expectations as application code. This ensures the repository remains the system of record for executable quality logic rather than a disconnected storage location for ad hoc scripts.

#### 4.3.3 Recording Pass/Fail Results
A test case or automated script will be marked Pass only when all expected assertions complete successfully without unresolved warnings that compromise the acceptance criterion being validated. For UI scenarios, this includes confirmation of correct control visibility, user feedback messaging, avatar rendering, and absence of full page reload behavior where prohibited. For API and integration scenarios, Pass requires compliant status codes, expected service sequencing, validated downstream handoffs, storage outcomes, cache invalidation events, and clean log signatures where applicable. For performance scenarios, Pass additionally requires the measured end-to-end time to remain within the 2.0-second SLA under the specified 4G network restriction.

A result will be marked Fail if any expected acceptance behavior is not achieved, if evidence is incomplete, if the environment materially distorts the scenario, or if a downstream control is bypassed even when the visible outcome appears correct. For example, a visible avatar update that occurs before malware scanning evidence is confirmed cannot be counted as Pass because it violates the required control sequence. Likewise, a successful file upload with an unsupported header, a delayed CDN sync, or a hidden UI that still allows unauthorized API submission must be recorded as Fail. Blocked will be used only when the test cannot reach a meaningful verdict due to environment outage, missing data, unavailable dependency, or unresolved build issue.

All results will be recorded with timestamp, environment, build version, executor, linked requirement, evidence location, and defect reference if failed. Automated executions will publish machine-readable results to CI dashboards and retain logs, screenshots, traces, and performance samples. Manual executions will capture comparable evidence in Jira-linked execution records. This consistent standard ensures pass/fail data supports release decisions, audit review, and root-cause analysis instead of serving as a superficial test-count metric.

### 4.4 Defect Tracking
Defect tracking will be managed in Jira using a controlled workflow that supports discovery, triage, remediation, verification, and closure. All defects raised during execution will include the affected build, environment, severity, priority, reproducibility, impacted acceptance criteria, technical domain, and complete supporting evidence such as screenshots, Playwright traces, API payloads, gateway logs, storage events, or CDN invalidation records. Each defect must be written so that engineering can reproduce it without informal follow-up, and QA must validate the clarity of reproduction steps before the issue enters active remediation. For AD-488, the defect taxonomy will emphasize security-impacting failures, sequence violations, stale-cache propagation issues, and performance breaches because those categories represent the most significant release threats.

The Jira workflow will follow a defined path such as New, Triage, In Progress, Ready for QA, Retest, Verified, Closed, or Reopened. Blocker and Critical issues will trigger immediate same-day triage with engineering leadership and may stop further progression in the release pipeline until disposition is agreed. High-severity issues that affect acceptance criteria, security, or SLA compliance must be resolved or formally risk-accepted before sign-off. Medium and Low issues may be deferred only if they do not compromise contractual acceptance, operational safety, or user trust and if product ownership explicitly accepts the residual risk. Reopened defects will require causal analysis to determine whether gaps exist in unit testing, peer review, automation scope, or deployment consistency.

Pull-request requirements are tightly integrated with this process. Any code change resolving a defect must reference the Jira issue, describe the fix domain, identify impacted automated tests, and include evidence that a targeted re-test plus surrounding regression have been executed. Peer reviewers must confirm not only that the fix addresses the obvious symptom, but also that adjacent risks such as token parsing, header validation, storage sequencing, or cache invalidation were considered. This approach prevents narrow fixes from introducing new regressions. Weekly defect trend reviews will analyze arrival rate, reopen rate, component hot spots, and escaped-risk indicators, feeding back into resource allocation and regression prioritization.

### 4.5 Test Data
Test data for this feature will be curated to reflect both expected business use and adversarial or boundary behavior. The primary data set includes authenticated test users with valid active sessions, accounts with expired or invalid tokens, and profiles with pre-existing avatars for overwrite and propagation verification. File payloads will include valid JPEG and WebP images below 5MB, exact 5.00MB payloads, 5.01MB and larger files, unsupported PNG/SVG/GIF/raw design assets, renamed text and script files with spoofed extensions, malformed headers, and simulated infected samples appropriate for controlled malware-scan validation. The data strategy is intentionally deterministic so that failures reflect system behavior rather than ambiguity in the source payloads.

Data creation and maintenance will be versioned and governed. Binary samples used in automation will be checksum-controlled where possible to ensure repeatability across runners and environments. Test accounts will be reset or reseeded between major cycles to avoid residual avatar state, stale session effects, or cached-view contamination. Sensitive or potentially executable samples used for security validation will be stored and accessed under restricted controls, with safe surrogates or sanctioned malware-test artifacts used instead of uncontrolled files. This balance allows rigorous negative testing without violating enterprise security standards.

