### 4.6 Test Environments
The strategy requires multiple environments aligned to execution purpose. Development environments will support early component and contract checks, including token-state simulation and basic API validation. The QA environment will be the principal integration workspace, containing API Gateway connectivity, deterministic malware-scan behavior, dedicated cloud bucket partitions, CDN observability, and browser/network tooling for UI and transport diagnostics. UAT will be used selectively for business confirmation of visible propagation and profile experience. Pre-production will provide the closest production-like validation point for end-to-end timing, routing, and cache behavior prior to release.

Each environment must provide browser automation support, request/response tracing, storage verification access, and the ability to apply standard 4G network throttling. Environment readiness criteria include stable deployment of the avatar feature, accessible observability dashboards, seeded accounts in required states, presence of approved file samples, and confirmation that cache invalidation can be observed or inferred through supported telemetry. The test team will not begin formal execution until a readiness checklist confirms build deployment, service connectivity, data availability, and logging health.

### 4.7 Test Automation Strategy
Automation for AD-488 will be built as a layered suite designed to provide fast feedback at pull-request time and high-confidence integration evidence before release. Playwright will be the primary framework for browser automation because it is well suited to asserting authentication-gated UI visibility, upload control behavior, crop-flow initiation, DOM refresh behavior, network interception, and the absence of full page reloads. Playwright traces and screenshots will be retained for every failed run, enabling rapid diagnosis of timing-sensitive propagation issues. PyTest will serve as the orchestration framework for API, gateway, storage, and data-validation checks, including assertions for 401 rejection handling, payload boundary responses, header inspection outcomes, bucket persistence verification, and service-sequence validation. This combination provides strong alignment to the architecture, allowing the team to validate browser and service layers independently while still composing end-to-end workflows.

JMeter will be used for SLA-focused performance validation, especially to simulate upload timing under standard 4G conditions and to measure the cumulative latency across gateway receipt, malware scanning, storage commit, CDN invalidation, and final accessibility of the updated asset. OWASP ZAP will support targeted security probing of the upload endpoint and gateway protections, helping verify improper content handling, request filtering, and exposure of unsafe behaviors beyond the defined negative test pack. tcconfig will be used to apply reproducible Linux network shaping in shared runners or dedicated test hosts so that the 4G restriction is not left to manual approximation. Together, these tools form an automation ecosystem that reflects the real risk profile of the feature rather than forcing all coverage into one framework.

CI/CD integration is mandatory. Pull requests affecting avatar functionality will trigger unit/configuration checks, PyTest API validations, and Playwright smoke scenarios. Merge to protected integration branches will trigger broader suites including boundary, negative, and contract tests. Nightly scheduled runs in QA will execute integrated regression with artifact retention, while pre-release pipelines in pre-production will run the full end-to-end suite plus JMeter timing analysis and any required ZAP security scans. Pipeline failures at merge-blocking stages will prevent progression until corrected or explicitly waived through approved governance. Peer review is equally important: all automation scripts must be reviewed for assertion quality, determinism, data handling, and traceability linkage before they become release-governing assets.

### 4.8 Test Results and Reporting
Test reporting will aggregate automated and manual execution outcomes into a single release view that emphasizes risk posture, not just volume statistics. Daily reports during active validation will show planned versus executed tests, pass/fail/blocked distribution, defect severity, impacted acceptance criteria, environment issues, and trend movement against release objectives. Reporting will differentiate between merge-blocking checks, nightly regression results, and formal release-certification evidence so stakeholders can understand both current quality state and confidence trajectory. For this feature, evidence summaries will include access-control coverage, file-boundary and format coverage, malware-sequence evidence, storage/CDN propagation evidence, and SLA timing results.

Stakeholder reporting will follow a tiered model. Engineering teams will receive detailed failure diagnostics with logs and traces; product and release management will receive concise risk summaries tied to business impact and open decision points. Human-in-the-loop release gates remain active, and pipeline state will be treated as Paused at Gate 3 pending manual validation of the final evidence package. Only after that validation will the release recommendation move forward. This preserves the requested governance expectation while still leveraging automation for data collection and repeatability.

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
Historical test execution logs, screenshots, traces, JMeter reports, API response captures, and environment-specific evidence will be stored in CI artifact repositories and linked from Jira execution records and release dashboards. Retention periods should align with enterprise audit policy, with release-certification evidence retained longer than routine branch-level smoke results. At minimum, all artifacts required to justify the production decision for AD-488 must remain accessible through the full release support window and any post-release incident review period.

To maintain integrity, artifacts will be tagged with build number, environment, branch, suite name, execution timestamp, and issue or test identifiers. Failed-run artifacts must never be overwritten by retries; instead, retries should generate linked records so diagnosis can distinguish transient instability from deterministic failure. This retention model supports trend analysis, defect reproduction, and governance reviews, especially for a feature involving security validation and latency-sensitive distributed updates.

