## 5. Resource Estimate
The projected resource model for this story is lean but cross-functional due to the number of integrated systems involved. Core participants include one QA lead to own strategy, risk, triage, and sign-off readiness; one QA automation engineer to implement and maintain Playwright and PyTest coverage; one performance engineer to design and run JMeter plus tcconfig-based SLA validations; one security engineer or security-focused QA resource to conduct OWASP ZAP and malware-path verification; one development representative for service trace analysis and defect turnaround; and one DevOps or release engineer to support pipeline configuration, environment readiness, and artifact retention. Product participation is required during UAT and risk acceptance decisions.

A realistic execution timeline for a feature of this complexity is approximately 8 to 10 working days once stable environments and data are available. Days 1 to 2 should cover test design refinement, traceability mapping, and environment/data validation. Days 2 to 5 should focus on automation updates and early execution across branch and QA environments. Days 5 to 7 should emphasize integrated end-to-end, malware-sequence, storage/CDN, and negative-path coverage. Days 7 to 8 should run SLA and network-constrained tests. Remaining time should support defect re-test, selective regression, evidence consolidation, and Gate 3 manual validation. Timeline risk increases materially if gateway tracing, malware mocks, or CDN observability are delayed.

## 6. Test Schedule / Milestone
A high-level execution schedule is defined to align delivery with risk reduction. Milestone 1 is strategy and traceability completion, including validation that Output1 and Output2 inputs are complete and unaltered for AD-488. Milestone 2 is environment readiness, where authentication fixtures, file packs, storage partitions, and observability access are confirmed. Milestone 3 is automation baseline execution in development and QA. Milestone 4 is full integration and negative-path execution in QA. Milestone 5 is pre-production SLA and release-candidate validation. Milestone 6 is defect closure review and Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 evidence approval.

Indicative durations are as follows: strategy and traceability 1 day; environment and data readiness 1 day; automation implementation or update 2 to 3 days; integrated functional and security execution 2 days; performance validation 1 day; re-test and focused regression 1 to 2 days; release review and sign-off 0.5 day. This schedule assumes no major environment outages and that critical defects are addressed within the same sprint window.

## 7. Entry and Exit Criteria
Entry criteria include approved story scope, available test environments, seeded accounts, validated sample files, CI/CD pipeline access, and confirmed observability for gateway, malware-scan, storage, and CDN layers. The build under test must be deployable and stable enough to support deterministic execution. Required automation baselines must be operational, and open blocking defects unrelated to this feature must not prevent meaningful validation.

Exit criteria require successful completion of all critical-path scenarios, evidence that authentication gating and security controls behave correctly, evidence that exact 5.00MB acceptance and 5.01MB rejection work as specified, confirmed storage and CDN invalidation sequencing, and demonstrated compliance with the 2.0-second SLA under standard 4G conditions. No Critical defects may remain open, and any residual High risk must have documented approval. Human-in-the-loop Gate 3 manual validation must explicitly confirm the release evidence before final promotion.

## 8. Roles and Responsibilities
The QA Lead owns the strategy, traceability, defect governance, daily reporting, and final quality recommendation. The QA Automation Engineer owns Playwright and PyTest implementation, pipeline reliability for automated suites, and failed-run diagnostics. The Performance Engineer owns JMeter scenario design, threshold analysis, and tcconfig-controlled network simulations. The Security Engineer owns OWASP ZAP execution, malware-path validation support, and security defect consultation. Engineering owners are responsible for code fixes, log interpretation, and peer review of risky implementation changes. DevOps is responsible for CI/CD configuration, environment stability support, branch protection enforcement, and artifact retention plumbing. Product and release stakeholders own acceptance of any residual non-blocking risk.

