Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation Actions | Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Authentication token expiration timing may create inconsistent outcomes between hidden UI state and gateway authorization, causing false positives in testing or escaped defects in production. | Medium | High | High | 1. Create deterministic valid, expired, and missing session fixtures. 2. Validate both DOM gating and direct API submission paths in the same cycle. 3. Add PyTest checks for 401 responses and Playwright checks for hidden or disabled controls. 4. Require peer review of token-handling changes and replay regression on every auth-related pull request. | QA Lead / Backend Engineer
R2 | Dynamic database or session-store connection drops during upload authorization or profile refresh could produce intermittent failures that masquerade as UI or CDN defects. | Medium | High | High | 1. Instrument gateway and session-validation logs for correlation IDs. 2. Tag failures by dependency domain during triage. 3. Add retry-aware diagnostics without masking product defects. 4. Maintain environment health checks before execution windows. 5. Escalate recurring infrastructure instability to DevOps for capacity and resilience review. | DevOps / Backend Engineer
R3 | Malware scan mock or controlled scan service desynchronization may allow storage to occur before scan completion, invalidating security evidence and release confidence. | Medium | Critical | Critical | 1. Use deterministic scan fixtures for clean and infected outcomes. 2. Capture event sequencing across gateway, scan service, and storage logs. 3. Add PyTest assertions that bucket writes do not occur before successful scan completion. 4. Block release if sequencing evidence is unavailable or ambiguous. 5. Re-run targeted regression after any scanner configuration change. | Security Test Engineer / Backend Engineer
R4 | CDN cache purge propagation latency may exceed expectations, leading to stale avatars in some active views despite successful storage and local UI refresh. | High | High | Critical | 1. Validate cache invalidation events and edge retrieval from multiple views. 2. Use Playwright to assert DOM update without full reload while also checking network responses. 3. Introduce pre-prod monitoring checkpoints for purge completion timing. 4. Add rollback or feature-hold decision if stale-cache behavior persists in release candidate testing. | QA Lead / CDN Owner
R5 | SLA breach under standard 4G conditions may occur due to cumulative gateway, malware-scan, storage, or CDN latency even when each subsystem appears individually acceptable. | Medium | High | High | 1. Use tcconfig to standardize network shaping. 2. Execute JMeter timed scenarios and correlate with service-level timestamps. 3. Break down end-to-end latency by subsystem to isolate bottlenecks. 4. Establish threshold alerts in pre-prod. 5. Prevent release if the 2.0-second requirement is not demonstrated with reproducible evidence. | Performance Engineer / Release Manager
R6 | Spoofed extension or malformed-header files may bypass shallow validation if header parsing libraries or gateway rules drift from expected configuration. | Medium | High | High | 1. Maintain a curated negative-file pack including renamed text and script files. 2. Run PyTest contract checks on validation responses for every related service change. 3. Add OWASP ZAP targeted probes to inspect unsafe acceptance patterns. 4. Require secure-code peer review for validation-library updates. | Security Test Engineer / Backend Engineer

